  "estimatedMinutes": 1,
  "statement": "Each of the letters \\(W,\\) \\(X,\\) \\(Y,\\) and \\(Z\\) represents a different integer in the set \\(\\{1,2,3,4\\},\\) but not necessarily in that order. They satisfy \\[\\dfrac{W}{X}-\\dfrac{Y}{Z}=1\\] What is the sum of \\(W\\) and \\(Y?\\)",
  "answerChoices": [
    {
      "label": "A",
      "text": "\\(3 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "B",
      "text": "\\(4 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "C",
      "text": "\\(5 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "D",
      "text": "\\(6 \\)"
    },
    {
      "label": "E",
      "text": "\\(7 \\)"
    }
  ],
  "answer": {
    "letter": "e",
    "value": "7"
  },
  "solutions": [
    {
      "id": "sol.amc-8-1998-p10.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "The only way to get a difference of \\(1\\) is\n\n\\[\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{4}{2}=1.\\]\n\nThus, \\(W=3\\) and \\(Y=4,\\) so \\(W+Y=7.\\)\n\nThus, the correct answer is E .",
      "hints": [
        "List possible fractions using \\(1,\\) \\(2,\\) \\(3,\\) and \\(4.\\)",
        "The larger fraction must exceed the smaller one by exactly \\(1.\\)"
      ],
